<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>1. Abstract and Type of Project (Software)</h2>
<p>The <b>RestroScan - Restaurant Food Ordering System</b> is developed using modern software engineering practices and follows an iterative and organic software development model. The development is divided into four phases: <b>Inception, Elaboration, Construction, and Transition</b>. In the Inception Phase, the project scope, objectives, and detailed requirements were identified and documented in the Software Requirements Specification (SRS), focusing on overall menu management, order processing, table QR scanning, and administrative functionalities. This phase established a strong foundation for design, development, and testing.</p>
<p>Within RestroScan, the modules specifically manage restaurant menus, table allocations, and dynamic offers. During the Elaboration Phase, the module was designed using structured models, including the Object Model, Dynamic Model, and Functional Model, ensuring structured workflows and module interactions. In the Construction Phase, the system was implemented using <b>React.js</b> for the frontend, <b>Django (Python)</b> for the backend, and <b>SQLite/Relational DB</b> for database management, followed by rigorous testing.</p>
<p>The project is classified as an organic type software project under the COCOMO model. The system provides a centralized, user-friendly platform for managing restaurant orders via QR codes, reducing manual effort while improving operational efficiency, accuracy, and customer experience.</p>

<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>4. Project Development Process Model Used (Methodology)</h2>
<p>In the RestroScan project, a modern software development methodology has been adopted, which is divided into two main stages: <b>Engineering Stage</b> and <b>Production Stage</b>. The Engineering Stage comprises the Inception Phase and the Elaboration Phase, whereas the Production Stage includes the Construction Phase and the Transition Phase.</p>
<p>Each phase follows an iterative approach. The <b>Inception Phase</b> focuses on understanding the problem domain and defining the solution space (SRS). The <b>Elaboration Phase</b> results in a detailed architectural design, using UML diagrams and object models.</p>
<p>In the <b>Construction Phase</b>, actual coding and testing of the system are performed (using React and Django). Finally, the <b>Transition Phase</b> delivers the final product after incorporating user feedback and performing final validations.</p>

<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>5. PROJECT'S PROCESS DOCUMENTATION</h2>
<h2>5.1 INCEPTION PHASE</h2>
<h3>5.1.1 Initial Description of Problem</h3>
<p><b>Problem Statement:</b> In many restaurants, food ordering is still managed manually or through uncoordinated legacy systems, leading to order processing delays, incorrect billing, and lack of visibility into table occupancies. Additionally, the absence of integrated platforms for core restaurant functions—such as digital menus, QR-based ordering, and dynamic offer tracking—creates operational inefficiencies.</p>
<p>RestroScan addresses these issues by offering a streamlined, web-based platform that ensures accurate order management, QR code table integration, and real-time processing. Through its modular design, the system enhances administrative efficiency and enables timely decision-making.</p>

<h3>5.1.2 Software Requirement Specification - SRS</h3>
<h4>5.1.2.1 Non-Functional Requirements</h4>
<ul>
    <li><b>Hardware Requirements:</b> RAM: Minimum 2GB. Storage: Minimum 1 GB of free space.</li>
    <li><b>Software Requirements:</b> Web Browser (Chrome, Firefox). Backend: Django (Python). Frontend: React.js.</li>
    <li><b>Communication Interfaces:</b> RESTful APIs via HTTP/HTTPS.</li>
</ul>

<h4>5.1.2.2 Functional Requirements</h4>
<ul>
    <li><b>User/Admin Authentication:</b> Role-based access for restaurant admins and customers. Secure password hashing.</li>
    <li><b>Menu Management:</b> Admins can add, view, update, or delete menu items and categories.</li>
    <li><b>Table Management:</b> System auto-generates QR codes for tables. Customers can scan to view the menu and order.</li>
    <li><b>Order Management:</b> Customers can place dine-in or parcel orders. Admins can update order status (Pending, Preparing, Ready, Delivered).</li>
    <li><b>Offers & Banners:</b> Admins can manage promotional banners and discount offers for menu items.</li>
</ul>

<h4>5.1.2.3 Performance & Security Requirements</h4>
<p><b>Performance:</b> The system supports concurrent users with response times < 1 second. Real-time updates reflect swiftly.</p>
<p><b>Security Features:</b> Django CSRF protection, SQL Injection protection via Django ORM, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) protection in React, and secure API endpoints.</p>

<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>5.2 ELABORATION PHASE (DETAIL DESIGN)</h2>
<h3>5.2.1 Object Model</h3>
<h4>5.2.1.1 Object Classes</h4>
<table>
    <tr><th>SNo.</th><th>Name of Object/Class</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>RestaurantManager</td><td>Handles restaurant details and core config.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>MenuManager</td><td>Manages categories, items, and pricing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>OrderProcessor</td><td>Handles cart, order placement, and status updates.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>TableManager</td><td>Manages table creation, allocation, and QR generation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>OfferManager</td><td>Manages discounts, validity periods, and banners.</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>5.2.1.2 Data Dictionary</h4>
<table>
    <tr><th>Class</th><th>Data Member</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    <tr><td rowspan="3">MenuItem</td><td>name</td><td>varchar</td><td>Name of the food item</td></tr>
    <tr><td>price</td><td>decimal</td><td>Base price</td></tr>
    <tr><td>is_available</td><td>boolean</td><td>Current stock status</td></tr>
    <tr><td rowspan="3">Order</td><td>total_price</td><td>decimal</td><td>Total order cost</td></tr>
    <tr><td>status</td><td>varchar</td><td>Pending, Preparing, Ready</td></tr>
    <tr><td>payment_status</td><td>varchar</td><td>Pending, Success, Failed</td></tr>
    <tr><td rowspan="2">Table</td><td>table_number</td><td>integer</td><td>Unique table identifier</td></tr>
    <tr><td>qr_code</td><td>image</td><td>Auto-generated QR code</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>5.2.1.6 Object / ER Diagram Representation</h4>
<p><i>The relationships are defined as follows:</i></p>
<ul>
    <li>A <b>Restaurant</b> has many <b>Categories</b> and <b>MenuItems</b> (1:N).</li>
    <li>A <b>MenuItem</b> belongs to a <b>Category</b> (N:1) and can have many <b>Offers</b> (1:N).</li>
    <li>An <b>Order</b> consists of multiple <b>OrderItems</b> (1:N).</li>
    <li>An <b>OrderItem</b> maps a <b>MenuItem</b> to a <b>Table</b>.</li>
</ul>

<div class="center">
    <div style="border:1px solid black; padding:10px; display:inline-block; margin: 10px;">Restaurant</div>
    <b>---(1:N)---></b>
    <div style="border:1px solid black; padding:10px; display:inline-block; margin: 10px;">MenuItem</div>
    <br>
    <div style="border:1px solid black; padding:10px; display:inline-block; margin: 10px;">Table</div>
    <b>---(1:N)---></b>
    <div style="border:1px solid black; padding:10px; display:inline-block; margin: 10px;">Order</div>
</div>

