<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>5.3 CONSTRUCTION PHASE</h2>
<h3>5.3.1 CASE Tools used to design</h3>
<p>Visual Studio Code (VS Code) was used as the primary IDE for coding both the frontend and backend.</p>

<h3>5.3.2 Coding Language and Operating System (OS) used</h3>
<p><b>OS:</b> Windows OS. <br><b>Backend:</b> Python using the Django Web Framework. <br><b>Frontend:</b> JavaScript using the React.js library. <br><b>Database:</b> SQLite (Django default) / Relational DB.</p>

<h3>5.3.3 Database connectivity procedure</h3>
<p>The backend utilizes Django's built-in Object-Relational Mapper (ORM). Database connectivity is configured in <code>settings.py</code> using the <code>DATABASES</code> dictionary, securely handling migrations and connections.</p>

<h3>5.3.4 Code description</h3>
<table>
    <tr><th>S.no</th><th>File name</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>models.py</td><td>Defines database schema (MenuItem, Order, Table)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>views.py / APIs</td><td>Handles RESTful logic for orders and menu fetching</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>App.js</td><td>React root component and routing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>CartPage.jsx</td><td>Handles shopping cart UI and checkout logic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>CheckoutPage.jsx</td><td>Manages order submission and payments</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>5.4 TRANSITION PHASE</h2>
<h3>5.4.1 Report of Alpha testing</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>Black-box Unit Testing:</b> Each API endpoint (e.g., adding an item, placing an order) was tested for expected JSON outputs.</li>
    <li><b>White-box Unit Testing:</b> Internal logic like discount calculation (<code>final_price</code> property) and QR generation were tested.</li>
    <li><b>Integrated System Testing:</b> End-to-end flow from scanning the QR code, viewing the menu, adding to cart, and confirming the order was validated.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5.4.2 DSLOC and Cost estimation</h3>
<p>Based on Intermediate COCOMO, the organic project required estimated efforts calculated using standard function points for user inputs (Menu, Orders), outputs (Receipts), and interfaces. Total estimated source lines of code (DSLOC) roughly amounts to 5-8 KLOC.</p>

<div class="pb"></div>
<h2>6. Limitations & Future Enhancements</h2>
<h3>6.1 Limitations</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>Static Payment Gateway:</b> Advanced third-party split payments are not fully dynamic.</li>
    <li><b>Scalability:</b> Caching mechanisms (like Redis) are needed for handling massive concurrent table connections.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6.2 Future Enhancements</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>AI Recommendations:</b> Suggesting food items based on previous orders and trending items.</li>
    <li><b>Real-time WebSockets:</b> Live order tracking from "Preparing" to "Delivered" without page refresh.</li>
    <li><b>Advanced Analytics:</b> Admin dashboards showing daily sales graphs and peak hours.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. Conclusion</h2>
<p>The RestroScan system successfully replaces manual food ordering with a centralized, digital solution. Developed through iterative phases (Inception, Elaboration, Construction, Transition), it utilizes a robust tech stack of React.js and Django. The system achieves its objectives of efficient menu management, seamless QR-based table ordering, and accurate billing, providing a strong foundation for future restaurant operational enhancements.</p>

